From 773d73be650f1059c136f188343065d16e4ce9b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 12:03:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(connectors): capture failure forensics and fix the six
 invalid selectors

Six selectors were jQuery syntax (`button:contains("Add")`). querySelector
rejects the whole string, so the valid alternatives listed beside them never
ran either, and the availability paths of Filmmakers, CastingNetwork, JobWork
and Wanted failed on every attempt regardless of what the sites looked like.

Rather than fix them blind - no connector has ever run against a live platform
and none can be reached from a development machine - this adds the evidence
first.

Forensics
  Every failure writes a full-page screenshot, the page HTML, the final URL and
  the title. A summary also goes into SyncLog.metadata and the log stream, so
  it is reachable without shell access to the container. Credentials are
  stripped from the HTML before it is written. Capture happens in the catch,
  before the finally that closes the browser - after close() there is no page
  left to photograph. Best-effort throughout: forensics must never be the
  reason an operation fails.

Selectors
  findByCssOrText tries structural selectors first and the button label second,
  in JavaScript rather than in the selector string. Fixing the four call sites
  also removed three paths that reported success without doing the work: two
  `if (addButton)` guards and a submit wrapped in a try commented "form might
  auto-save". All three now throw PlatformChangedError.

Checks
  check-selectors.mjs runs all 70 selectors through real Chromium and fails on
  any that are not valid CSS. Its first version reported everything valid while
  the six broken ones were still present, because a regex had silently
  extracted 11 of 70 - it now walks the source with a quote-aware scanner, and
  was verified by reintroducing a known-bad selector and watching it go red.
  check-text-selectors.mjs exercises the text helpers against fixture markup.

Also fixed
  page.url() is synchronous; the .catch() on it threw and silently discarded
  every capture. npm ci was broken in backend/ - the lock had drifted from
  package.json - so the lock is regenerated and the Dockerfile now uses
  npm ci --omit=dev, which fails the build on future drift instead of quietly
  installing an untested tree.

Valid CSS is not a working selector. Nothing here has touched a live platform,
and the German text fallbacks are guesses at the real button copy.
